# SQL Server & T-SQL Standards

## General Principles
- Prioritize readability over brevity
- Write deterministic, predictable queries
- Avoid overly complex queries

## Formatting
- Use uppercase for SQL keywords
- Align clauses for readability

Example:
SELECT Column1, Column2
FROM TableName
WHERE IsActive = 1
ORDER BY CreatedDate DESC;

## Naming Conventions
- Use PascalCase for:
  - Tables
  - Columns
  - Stored Procedures
- Use meaningful names (no abbreviations unless standard)

## Aliases
- Use short, meaningful aliases
- Avoid single-letter aliases unless obvious

## Joins
- Always use explicit JOIN syntax (no implicit joins)
- Clearly define join conditions

## Filtering
- Always specify columns in SELECT (avoid SELECT *)
- Filter as early as possible

## Performance
- Avoid:
  - SELECT *
  - Cursors (unless absolutely necessary)
  - Scalar functions in WHERE clauses
- Use indexes effectively
- Analyze execution plans

## Pagination
- Use OFFSET/FETCH for paging

## Transactions
- Keep transactions short
- Use TRY/CATCH for error handling

## Error Handling
- Use TRY...CATCH blocks
- Return meaningful error messages

## Stored Procedures
- Use for complex operations or performance-critical logic
- Keep them focused and single-purpose

## Parameterization
- Always use parameters (prevent SQL injection)
- Avoid dynamic SQL unless necessary

## Temp Tables vs CTEs
- Use CTEs for readability
- Use temp tables when:
  - Reuse is needed
  - Performance requires it

## Index Awareness
- Ensure queries align with indexes
- Avoid functions on indexed columns in WHERE

## NULL Handling
- Be explicit with NULL checks
- Use IS NULL / IS NOT NULL (not = NULL)

## Dates
- Use ISO formats (YYYY-MM-DD)
- Avoid ambiguous date formats

## EF Core Considerations
- Write SQL that EF can translate efficiently
- Avoid patterns that force client-side evaluation

## Security
- Never concatenate SQL strings with user input
- Use parameterized queries or stored procedures

## Maintainability
- Break complex queries into steps
- Use comments only to explain WHY, not WHAT